--TEST--
`=`, `⊕=`, an increment and `unset()` agree on an element target whose holder is no slot, and each takes exactly one diagnostic — ADR 0007 § 5, ADR 0014 § 1, ADR 0036 § 4
--FILE--
<?mwl
// ADR 0007 § 5 separates an array before an element of it is written, and the
// separated copy has to land back in whatever held it. Three holders have no
// slot for it to land in — `?->`, whose `null` is not a place, ADR 0014 § 1's
// hooked property, which is a pair of accessors rather than storage, and ADR
// 0036 § 4's erased property, resolved by name at run time. Four spellings
// write through a holder: a plain `=`, a compound `⊕=`, an increment, and
// `unset()`, which removes an entry by separating the array exactly as a write
// does. What this pins is that the four *agree* — the same three refusals in
// the same order, four times over, and exactly one diagnostic per line, so a
// spelling that grew its own answer, or that let `E0482` blame the subscript
// for the holder the very next code names, fails here while still reading
// right on its own line.
class Plain {
    public array<int> $counts;

    public function constructor() {
        array<int> $seed = ["0" => 1];
        $this->counts = $seed;
    }
}

class Grid {
    public array<int> $counts {
        get => $this->counts;
        set (array<int> $v) {
            $this->counts = $v;
        }
    }

    public function constructor() {
        array<int> $seed = ["0" => 1];
        $this->counts = $seed;
    }
}

?Plain $maybe = new Plain();
var $g = new Grid();
object $erased = new Plain();

$maybe?->counts["0"] = 5;
$g->counts["0"] = 5;
$erased->counts["0"] = 5;

$maybe?->counts["0"] += 5;
$g->counts["0"] += 5;
$erased->counts["0"] += 5;

$maybe?->counts["0"]++;
$g->counts["0"]++;
$erased->counts["0"]++;

unset($maybe?->counts["0"]);
unset($g->counts["0"]);
unset($erased->counts["0"]);

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
